{
 "lead": "A coherent receiver needs a local carrier locked to the phase of the incoming one. When the oscillators drift, or the receiver must be cheap, non-coherent detection does without that phase. For FSK and OOK it measures envelopes; for phase keying it puts the data in the change of phase from one bit to the next, which is differential phase shift keying (DPSK). The price is a small loss in error probability, often under 1 dB.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#ask-and-fsk-generation-and-detection\">ASK and FSK</a> and <a href=\"#bpsk-generation-detection-and-error-probability\">BPSK</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Non-coherent BFSK and OOK",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The envelope of a bandpass signal is its slowly varying amplitude. It is never negative and does not change when the carrier phase changes, so a receiver that looks only at envelopes needs no phase reference."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Pass the received signal through two bandpass filters, one centred on f<sub>1</sub> and one on f<sub>0</sub> (or use I and Q correlators at each tone and add their squares).",
      "Envelope-detect each filter output.",
      "Sample both envelopes at the end of the bit.",
      "Decide 1 if the f<sub>1</sub> envelope is larger, otherwise 0."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The tones must be orthogonal for every phase difference, which needs a spacing that is a whole multiple of R<sub>b</sub> = 1/T<sub>b</sub>: at least 1/T<sub>b</sub>, twice the coherent minimum (see <a href=\"#ask-and-fsk-generation-and-detection\">ASK and FSK</a>)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Non-coherent BFSK:  P<sub>b</sub> = ½ e<sup>−E<sub>b</sub>/2N<sub>0</sub></sup></b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "E<sub>b</sub> is the energy per bit in joules and N<sub>0</sub> the one-sided noise density in W/Hz."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Non-coherent OOK uses one bandpass filter, an envelope detector and a threshold near half the received amplitude. At high E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> its error probability is close to the same ½e<sup>−E<sub>b</sub>/2N<sub>0</sub></sup>, with E<sub>b</sub> the <i>average</i> energy per bit (half the energy of an on burst). Its best threshold moves with the signal level, a weakness in fading."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "DPSK: the data rides on phase changes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "DPSK encodes each bit b<sub>k</sub> into a transmitted bit d<sub>k</sub> that depends on the previous transmitted bit:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>d<sub>k</sub> = b<sub>k</sub> ⊕ d<sub>k−1</sub></b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "⊕ is exclusive OR and d<sub>0</sub> is a known reference bit. Then d<sub>k</sub> = 1 is sent with phase π and d<sub>k</sub> = 0 with phase 0. A data 1 flips the phase and a data 0 keeps it. (Some books use the complement, where a 1 keeps the phase; the receiver must match.)"
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Split the received signal into a direct path and a path delayed by exactly T<sub>b</sub>.",
      "Multiply the two (or, in the optimum form, correlate each bit with the previous bit's I and Q outputs).",
      "Integrate or lowpass-filter over the bit.",
      "A negative result means the phase changed, so b<sub>k</sub> = 1; a positive result means b<sub>k</sub> = 0."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The previous bit acts as the phase reference, so the receiver needs no carrier recovery, only a phase that stays steady over two bits. A constant phase offset, even 180°, cancels in the comparison, which also removes the 180° ambiguity of a BPSK carrier loop. For this receiver in white Gaussian noise the result is exact, not an approximation:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>DPSK:  P<sub>b</sub> = ½ e<sup>−E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub></sup></b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Because the reference is itself noisy, DPSK is slightly worse than coherent BPSK, and one wrong bit spoils two decisions, so errors tend to come in pairs."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Choosing between the simple receivers",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Scheme",
      "Phase reference",
      "P<sub>b</sub>",
      "E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> for 10<sup>−5</sup>"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Coherent BPSK",
       "recovered carrier",
       "Q(√(2E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>))",
       "9.59 dB"
      ],
      [
       "DPSK",
       "previous bit",
       "½e<sup>−E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub></sup>",
       "10.34 dB"
      ],
      [
       "Coherent BFSK",
       "recovered carrier",
       "Q(√(E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>))",
       "12.60 dB"
      ],
      [
       "Non-coherent BFSK",
       "none",
       "½e<sup>−E<sub>b</sub>/2N<sub>0</sub></sup>",
       "13.35 dB"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "DPSK costs about 0.75 dB against BPSK at P<sub>b</sub> = 10<sup>−5</sup>; non-coherent BFSK costs about 0.75 dB against coherent BFSK. Both gaps shrink as E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> rises. Non-coherent BFSK suits cheap sensor and pager links; DPSK suits links whose phase drifts slowly, such as some optical and satellite systems; fast phase noise that changes the phase within two bits breaks DPSK."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A DPSK transmitter uses d<sub>k</sub> = b<sub>k</sub> ⊕ d<sub>k−1</sub> with reference d<sub>0</sub> = 0, phase 0 for d = 0 and π for d = 1. Encode the data 1 1 0 1, list the transmitted phases, and show the receiver's decisions. Then rotate every received phase by 180° and decode again.",
   "steps": [
    "d<sub>1</sub> = 1 ⊕ 0 = 1; d<sub>2</sub> = 1 ⊕ 1 = 0; d<sub>3</sub> = 0 ⊕ 0 = 0; d<sub>4</sub> = 1 ⊕ 0 = 1.",
    "Sent sequence including the reference: d = 0 | 1 0 0 1, phases 0 | π 0 0 π.",
    "Receiver compares each phase with the one before: π−0 changed → 1; 0−π changed → 1; 0−0 same → 0; π−0 changed → 1. Decoded 1 1 0 1.",
    "Rotated by 180°: phases π | 0 π π 0. Changes: changed, changed, same, changed. Decoded 1 1 0 1 again.",
    "Check with XOR: b<sub>k</sub> = d<sub>k</sub> ⊕ d<sub>k−1</sub> = 1⊕0, 0⊕1, 0⊕0, 1⊕0 = 1 1 0 1."
   ],
   "answer": "Encoded 1 0 0 1 after the reference 0; phases 0 | π 0 0 π. The decisions give 1 1 0 1 both before and after a 180° rotation, because only phase changes carry data."
  },
  {
   "question": "At E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 10 dB, find P<sub>b</sub> for coherent BPSK, DPSK, coherent BFSK and non-coherent BFSK, and rank them.",
   "steps": [
    "E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 10 (linear).",
    "BPSK: Q(√20) = Q(4.472) = 3.87 × 10<sup>−6.</sup>",
    "DPSK: ½e<sup>−10</sup> = 0.5 × 4.54 × 10<sup>−5</sup> = 2.27 × 10<sup>−5.</sup>",
    "Coherent BFSK: Q(√10) = Q(3.162) = 7.83 × 10<sup>−4.</sup>",
    "Non-coherent BFSK: ½e<sup>−5</sup> = 0.5 × 6.74 × 10<sup>−3</sup> = 3.37 × 10<sup>−3.</sup>",
    "Check: each step down the list needs a phase reference less or spends the 3 dB of orthogonal signalling, and each is worse."
   ],
   "answer": "BPSK 3.87 × 10<sup>−6</sup> &lt; DPSK 2.27 × 10<sup>−5</sup> &lt; coherent BFSK 7.83 × 10<sup>−4</sup> &lt; non-coherent BFSK 3.37 × 10<sup>−3.</sup>"
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Non-coherent BFSK tones need a spacing of n/T<sub>b</sub> (at least R<sub>b</sub>), not n/(2T<sub>b</sub>).",
  "The exponents differ: DPSK is ½e<sup>−E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub></sup>; non-coherent BFSK and OOK are ½e<sup>−E<sub>b</sub>/2N<sub>0</sub></sup>.",
  "DPSK needs a known reference bit d<sub>0</sub> at the start, and the receiver must use the same XOR convention as the transmitter.",
  "Do not decode DPSK from absolute phases; only the change between neighbouring bits carries data, so a 180° carrier slip does no harm.",
  "½e<sup>−E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub></sup> is the exact DPSK result in white Gaussian noise, not a high-SNR approximation."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Give the P<sub>b</sub> of DPSK and of non-coherent BFSK.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "DPSK ½e<sup>−E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub></sup>; non-coherent BFSK ½e<sup>−E<sub>b</sub>/2N<sub>0</sub></sup>."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why can an envelope detector ignore the carrier phase?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "The envelope is the magnitude of the bandpass signal; a phase shift rotates the carrier but leaves the magnitude unchanged."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Non-coherent BFSK runs at T<sub>b</sub> = 1 ms. Find the minimum tone spacing.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "1/T<sub>b</sub> = 1 kHz."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Encode 0 1 1 in DPSK with d<sub>k</sub> = b<sub>k</sub> ⊕ d<sub>k−1</sub> and d<sub>0</sub> = 0.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "d = 0, 1, 0, so the sent bits are 0 | 0 1 0."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Find P<sub>b</sub> for DPSK at E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 8 (linear).",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "½e<sup>−8</sup> = 0.5 × 3.35 × 10<sup>−4</sup> = 1.68 × 10<sup>−4.</sup>"
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Why is DPSK worse than coherent BPSK at the same E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>, and why do its errors come in pairs?",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "Its reference is the previous received bit, which carries its own noise, so the comparison is noisier than with a clean local carrier. A badly received bit is the reference for the next decision as well, so it can spoil two decisions."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "A link's oscillator drifts by a few degrees per second at 10 kb/s. Would DPSK work? Why?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Yes: over two bits (200 µs) the phase moves by a tiny fraction of a degree, so the previous bit is a good reference."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. Differential detection reappears as π/4-DQPSK in older mobile standards and as DPSK and DQPSK with delay-line interferometers in optical links. Modern receivers usually prefer pilot symbols and digital phase tracking, which recover most of the gap to coherent detection. Search next: DQPSK, delay interferometer, non-coherent FSK receivers.",
 "figure": {
  "after_section": 1,
  "caption": "Bit error probability against E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> for the four binary receivers. At 10 dB (dashed line, the second worked example) the order is BPSK, DPSK, coherent BFSK, non-coherent BFSK; DPSK and non-coherent BFSK each sit less than 1 dB to the right of their coherent partners.",
